From: devvm <https://github.com/bindreams/devvm>
Subject: [PATCH] target/i386/whpx: take the XSAVE area size from the hypervisor

QEMU answers CPUID[0xD,0].EBX, the XSAVE area size for the features enabled in
XCR0, from its own stale copy of XCR0. The hypervisor already supplies the
current value in DefaultResultRbx, which QEMU takes for sub-leaves 1 and 2. A
guest then sees an area smaller than XSAVE writes (measured on an AMD Zen 5
host, Windows guest at XCR0=0xE7: 576 bytes instead of 2432), and software that
sizes a buffer from it, such as Cygwin 3.6, overruns it. CPUID reads only EAX
and ECX, so the leaf and sub-leaf are compared as 32-bit values.

To be upstreamed; applies after 0002.
---
--- a/target/i386/whpx/whpx-all.c
+++ b/target/i386/whpx/whpx-all.c
@@ -2659,10 +2659,9 @@
                 }
             }
 
-            /* CPUID[0xD,{1,2}].EBX are dynamic depending on guest features. */
-            if (vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.Rax == 0xd) {
-                if (vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.Rcx == 1
-                    || vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.Rcx == 2) {
+            /* CPUID[0xD,0].EBX depends on XCR0 and [0xD,1].EBX on XCR0|XSS. */
+            if ((uint32_t)vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.Rax == 0xd) {
+                if ((uint32_t)vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.Rcx <= 2) {
                     reg_values[4].Reg64 = vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.DefaultResultRbx;
                 }
             }
